@nov21_sajbor
title: “Not one more”
new-page: true
view-if: november_2021_sajbor_done = 0 and abortion_law_enacted = 0 and abortion_reform_stage = 0
on-arrival: november_2021_sajbor_done = 1

= The law inside a hospital

**Izabela Sajbor** was thirty, in her twenty-second week, and admitted to the
county hospital in Pszczyna after her waters broke. The scans showed the foetus
had no chance of surviving. She was kept under observation while the doctors
waited — for a heartbeat to stop, for a legal certainty the Tribunal ruling had
taken away from them — and she died of septic shock with her family's messages
still on her telephone.

Her sister publishes them. *They can't do anything as long as it's alive,
thanks to the anti-abortion law.* The hospital issues a statement about
following procedures. It is, in the narrowest sense, true.

Every obstetrician in the country now understands the position exactly. A
prosecutor may examine a decision to terminate; nobody has ever been prosecuted
for waiting. The ruling did not have to ban a procedure to end it — it only had
to make the doctor carry the risk.

Her name is on placards in forty cities by the weekend. The organisers have not
asked any party for permission and are not inclined to grant any.

- @nov21_protocols
- @nov21_march
- @nov21_party

